</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Inorganic_Chemistry/Inorganic_Chemistry_(LibreTexts)/09:_Coordination_Chemistry_I_-_Structure_and_Isomers/9.03:_Nomenclature_and_Ligands" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="Rule_4:_Specify_the_identity_of_the_metal"></span><h3 class="lt-chem-151409 editable">Rule 4: Specify the identity of the metal</h3>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-151409">In neutral and cationic complexes the metal's name is used directly</li>
</ul>

<p class="mt-indent-1 lt-chem-151409">- <em>e.g.</em> as in hexammine<u>ruthenium</u>(III) for [Ru(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup></p>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-151409">In anionic complexes, -<em>ate</em> replaces -<em>ium</em>, -<em>en</em>, or &ndash;<em>ese </em>or adds to the metal name,</li>
</ul>

<p class="mt-indent-1 lt-chem-151409">e.g., as in hexachloromanganate(IV) for MnCl<sub>6</sub><sup>2</sup><sup>-</sup></p>

<ul>
    <li class="lt-chem-151409">In anionic complexes of some metals a Latin-derived name is used instead of the element's English name. These names are given in Table \(\sf{\PageIndex{5}}\).</li>
</ul>

